{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The CLI does use `clap`, but the {impl} marking is incomplete and nothing is marked as a {test} for this requirement.\n\n**What is in place**\n- `Cargo.toml:42` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `src/main.rs:1` imports `clap::{ArgGroup, Parser, Subcommand}`.\n- `src/main.rs:10-16` (`struct[Cli]`, marked {impl}) is a `#[derive(Parser)]` root with `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]` and a subcommand field.\n- `src/main.rs:88-90` (`fn[main]`) calls `Cli::parse()`, marked {impl}.\n- No manual argument parsing exists. A search for `env::args` and `[[bin]]` found no other entry point.\n\n**Gap 1: the command and option definitions are not marked {impl}.**\n- The requirement says the CLI uses clap \"to define and parse its commands and options.\"\n- The definitions are the `Command` enum (`src/main.rs:18-76`), `MdbookCommand` (`78-81`) and `HookCommand` (`83-86`).\n- They carry the clap derive attributes: `#[derive(Subcommand)]`, `#[command(...)]`, `#[arg(...)]`, `ArgGroup`, `alias = \"author-evidence\"`, `hide = true`, and the `--item`/`--requirements` and `--for-requirement`/`--for-example` groups.\n- None of these has an {impl} reference to `requirements.cli.clap`.\n- `references` lists only two {impl} sites, `Cli` and `main`. The definition half of the requirement is therefore only partly attributed.\n- The `Cli` derive only reaches these enums indirectly through `command: Command`.\n\n**Gap 2: no test is marked for this requirement.**\n- `references` shows no {test} site for `requirements.cli.clap`.\n- Other tests exercise clap behaviour, but they are marked for other requirements:\n  - `tests/cli.rs` checks `Usage: habeas` on invalid input, marked `cli.invocation`.\n  - `tests/commands.rs` checks `--help` output and invalid arguments, marked `cli.status` and `cli.info`.\n- Nothing is marked as verifying the clap requirement itself.\n- A small test would have been practical, for example one asserting that `habeas --help` or a bad subcommand produces clap-style help or usage. The absence is not justified by impracticality.\n\nThere are no FIXME acknowledgements for either gap, so the determination is false rather than FIXME.",
  "inputs": [
